==English==

===Alternative spellings===
* '''[[femi-Nazi]]''', '''[[femme-Nazi]]'''

===Etymology===
[[portmanteau_word|Portmanteau]] of ''[[feminist]]'' and ''[[Nazi]]'', popularized by politically conservative radio personality [[w:Rush Limbaugh|Rush Limbaugh]], who credited his friend Tom Hazlett, a professor of law and economics at George Mason University, with coining the term.<ref>Rush H. Limbaugh, ''The Way Things Ought to Be'', Pocket Books, 1992, p.193]</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|US|pejorative|derogatory}} A [[radical]] or [[militant]] [[feminist]], perceived to be [[intolerant]] of opposing views.

===Usage notes===
* Limbaugh has specified that the term refers to "a feminist to whom the most important thing in life is ensuring that as many abortions as possible occur," saying at one point that, "There are fewer than twenty-five known Feminazis in the United States."<ref>Rush H. Limbaugh, ''The Way Things Ought to Be'', Pocket Books, 1992, p.296</ref><ref>Britt Gillette, ''The Dittohead's Guide to Adult Beverages'', Regnery Publishing, 2005, [http://books.google.com/books?id=ZsYroxqspX4C&pg=PR12&dq=Feminazi page xii]</ref> However, in practice, the term has been applied in a wider context referring to feminists generally on the radio show, and the term has taken on an even wider context in the general public, sometimes as a derogatory way to refer to a lesbian, feminist, or opinionated woman.

